[1mzfsbootmenu-luks[0m

  [1mSYNOPSIS[0m

    ZFSBootMenu kernel command line:

    zbm.luks=off|auto|device[,device...]
    zbm.luks_key=path[:device]
    zbm.luks_tokens=0|1
    zbm.luks_token_wait=seconds

    Build options (dracut configuration or ZFSBootMenu [33mmkinitcpio.conf[0m):

    zfsbootmenu_luks=yes|no
    zfsbootmenu_luks_crypttab=path
    zfsbootmenu_luks_tokens=yes|no
    zfsbootmenu_luks_token_plugins=yes|no
    zfsbootmenu_luks_clevis=yes|no
    zfsbootmenu_luks_gpg=yes|no
    zfsbootmenu_luks_gpg_keys="keyid ..."
    zfsbootmenu_luks_gpg_pubkeys="file ..."
    zfsbootmenu_luks_pcscd=yes|no
    zfsbootmenu_luks_yubikey=yes|no

  [1mDESCRIPTION[0m

    ZFSBootMenu can import ZFS pools whose vdevs are stored on LUKS (dm-crypt) volumes.
    Every vdev type is supported: data vdevs of any layout (single disks, mirrors,
    RAIDZ, dRAID), special, dedup, log, cache and spare vdevs, on whole disks or on
    partitions, alone or mixed with unencrypted vdevs. LUKS can be combined with ZFS
    native encryption.

    During start-up, ZFSBootMenu loads the ZFS modules, configures the hostid, waits for any
    devices named by [1mzbm.wait_for[0m, runs early-setup hooks, then unlocks LUKS volumes
    before it imports pools. Right before it starts the selected boot environment with
    [1mkexec[0m(8), it exports the pools on those volumes and closes them, which wipes the
    volume keys from kernel memory. The boot environment's own initramfs then unlocks
    the volumes again.

    A volume can be unlocked with a passphrase, a key file (plain, on another device, or
    GnuPG-encrypted), a key script, a TPM 2.0 chip, a FIDO2 security key, a clevis
    policy, an OpenPGP smart card, or YubiKey challenge-response. The LUKS passphrase
    always remains available as a fallback.

    The [1mzluks[0m(8) command manages LUKS volumes from the recovery shell or over SSH.

  [1mVOLUME SELECTION[0m

    The volumes to unlock are chosen by the first of these that applies:

      1. [1mzbm.luks[0m on the kernel command line;
      2. the crypttab embedded in the image, see zfsbootmenu-crypttab(5);
      3. automatic discovery: every LUKS volume on the system.

    Automatically discovered volumes are mapped as [33m/dev/mapper/luks-[0m[33mUUID[0m. After unlocking, a
    discovered volume whose contents are recognized as something other than a ZFS vdev,
    such as an ext4 file system or swap, is closed again.

  [1mUNLOCK SEQUENCE[0m

    For each volume, ZFSBootMenu tries the following methods in order and stops at the first
    one that opens the volume:

      1. a key script ([1mkeyscript=[0m);
      2. a key file, plain or GnuPG-encrypted;
      3. LUKS2 tokens. Tokens that need no interaction come first: TPM2 without a PIN, then
      clevis. Tokens that need interaction follow: TPM2 with a PIN, then FIDO2
      security keys;
      4. passphrases, and YubiKey responses, that unlocked an earlier volume in this run;
      5. YubiKey challenge-response ([1mykchalresp[0m);
      6. a passphrase prompt.

    Every prompt can be answered with an empty line to move on to the next method. An empty
    answer at the final passphrase prompt skips the volume.

    Secrets are reused within one unlock run, so the members of a mirror or RAIDZ vdev ask
    only once:

      • a passphrase or YubiKey response that opened one volume is tried on the others;
      • a TPM2 PIN that worked is tried once on other PIN-protected TPM2 tokens;
      • a GnuPG key file is decrypted only once.

    All of these live in RAM only, in a subshell, and are wiped when the run ends.

    When a volume is unlocked from elsewhere while a prompt is waiting, for example with
    [1mzluks[0m over SSH, the prompt notices and moves on.

  [1mCOMMAND-LINE PARAMETERS[0m

    These options are given on the ZFSBootMenu kernel command line, for example with
    [1mzbm-kcl[0m(8) or with [33mKernel.CommandLine[0m in [1mgenerate-zbm[0m(5).

    [1mzbm.luks=off[0m

      Do not unlock LUKS volumes.

    [1mzbm.luks=auto[0m

      Use automatic discovery, even when the image has a crypttab.

    [1mzbm.luks=[0m[33mdevice[0m[,[33mdevice[0m...]

      Unlock only the listed devices. Each [33mdevice[0m takes the forms described for the [33mdevice[0m
      field in zfsbootmenu-crypttab(5).

    [1mzbm.luks_key=[0m[33mpath[0m[:[33mdevice[0m]

      A key file for every volume that has none of its own. With [33mdevice[0m, the key file is
      read from that device, which is mounted read-only for the purpose. Files ending
      in [33m.gpg[0m, [33m.asc[0m or [33m.pgp[0m are decrypted with GnuPG.

    [1mzbm.luks_tokens=0[0m

      Do not use LUKS2 tokens.

    [1mzbm.luks_token_wait=[0m[33mseconds[0m

      How long to wait for a FIDO2 security key, OpenPGP smart card or YubiKey to be plugged
      in before moving on. The default is 10 seconds. Pressing any key ends the wait.

  [1mUNLOCK METHODS[0m

    [1mTPM2[0m

      LUKS2 tokens created with [1msystemd-cryptenroll --tpm2-device[0m, with or without
      [1m--tpm2-with-pin[0m, and optionally bound to PCRs. When the image contains systemd's
      [33msystemd-tpm2[0m cryptsetup plugin, the plugin unlocks the token. Otherwise
      ZFSBootMenu unseals it with [1mtpm2-tools[0m. The native implementation handles tokens
      created by systemd 254 or later, with or without a PIN and with or without a PCR
      policy. Signed PCR policies ([1m--tpm2-public-key[0m), [1m--tpm2-pcrlock[0m and tokens
      created by older systemd versions need the plugin.

      When the TPM refuses PINs after too many wrong attempts (dictionary-attack lockout),
      ZFSBootMenu says so and moves on instead of asking for a PIN it cannot use.

    [1mFIDO2[0m

      LUKS2 tokens created with [1msystemd-cryptenroll --fido2-device[0m for security keys with
      the [33mhmac-secret[0m extension, such as YubiKey 5 and Nitrokey 3. ZFSBootMenu waits
      for the key, asks for its PIN if the token requires one, and asks for a touch.
      Without systemd's [33msystemd-fido2[0m plugin, ZFSBootMenu computes the secret with
      [1mfido2-assert[0m(1).

    [1mclevis[0m

      LUKS2 tokens created with [1mclevis luks bind[0m. TPM2 policies work in every image that
      contains clevis. Tang policies need networking in ZFSBootMenu.

    [1mGnuPG-encrypted key files[0m

      A key file encrypted with [1mgpg --encrypt[0m to an OpenPGP key, typically one stored on a
      YubiKey or Nitrokey, or with [1mgpg --symmetric[0m. For keys on a card, ZFSBootMenu
      first reads the card and checks that it holds the key the file is encrypted to.
      Only then does it ask for the card PIN, showing the number of attempts left. The
      public keys come from [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_gpg_keys[0m or [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_gpg_pubkeys[0m.
      GnuPG's built-in CCID driver handles common readers; [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_pcscd=yes[0m
      adds [1mpcscd[0m(8) for others.

    [1mYubiKey challenge-response[0m

      The scheme used by [1myubikey-luks[0m: the typed passphrase (or its SHA-256) is sent to an
      HMAC-SHA1 slot, and the response (optionally appended to the challenge) is a
      LUKS passphrase. It is enabled per volume with the [1mykchalresp[0m crypttab options.

    [1mKey files and key scripts[0m

      Plain key files present in the running image or on another device, and Debian-style
      key scripts; see zfsbootmenu-crypttab(5).

  [1mLATE DEVICES[0m

    Devices named in the crypttab or in [1mzbm.luks[0m are waited for at start-up. Disks that
    appear later, such as slow USB enclosures or some SAS controllers, are handled by
    the pool-import retry loop. Before each import retry, ZFSBootMenu looks for LUKS
    volumes that are neither open nor skipped by the user, unlocks them, and retries the
    import at once.

  [1mBOOT ENVIRONMENT HAND-OFF[0m

    The system teardown hook [33m80-luks-close.sh[0m runs right before [1mkexec[0m. It exports every pool
    that has a vdev on a mapping opened by ZFSBootMenu, including vdevs on partitions
    inside a mapping, and closes the mappings. It can be disabled with
    [1mzbm.skip_hooks=80-luks-close.sh[0m.

    The boot environment must unlock its LUKS volumes again from its own initramfs, for
    example with a key file embedded in that initramfs. Because the initramfs is stored
    inside the encrypted pool, this does not expose the key.

  [1mBUILD OPTIONS[0m

    These options are set in a dracut configuration file in the directory named by
    [33mGlobal.DracutConfDir[0m in [1mgenerate-zbm[0m(5), or in the ZFSBootMenu [33mmkinitcpio.conf[0m.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks=no[0m

      Leave out LUKS support. By default it is included whenever [1mcryptsetup[0m is installed on
      the build host.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_crypttab=[0m[33mpath[0m

      The crypttab to embed. The default is [33m/etc/zfsbootmenu/crypttab[0m.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_tokens=no[0m

      Leave out TPM2, FIDO2 and clevis support.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_token_plugins=no[0m

      Leave out systemd's cryptsetup token plugins; ZFSBootMenu's own TPM2 and FIDO2 code is
      used instead.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_clevis=no[0m

      Leave out clevis.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_gpg=no[0m

      Leave out GnuPG.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_gpg_keys=[0m"[33mkeyid[0m ..."

      Export these OpenPGP public keys from the build host's keyring into the image.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_gpg_pubkeys=[0m"[33mfile[0m ..."

      Import the OpenPGP public keys in these files into the image.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_pcscd=yes[0m

      Include [1mpcscd[0m and its card-reader drivers.

    [1mzfsbootmenu_luks_yubikey=no[0m

      Leave out [1mykchalresp[0m.

    Each optional component is included only when it is installed on the build host. The
    image also receives [1mcryptsetup[0m, [1mdmsetup[0m, the device-mapper udev rules, [33mdm-crypt[0m and
    common crypto modules, and TPM, USB and HID drivers. The dracut [33mcrypt[0m module is left
    out of ZFSBootMenu images, and [33mrd.luks=0[0m is embedded as a safeguard, so that dracut
    does not prompt for LUKS volumes on its own.

  [1mFILES[0m

    [33m/etc/zfsbootmenu/crypttab[0m

      Volumes to unlock (on the build host, and in the image).

    [33m/etc/zfsbootmenu/gnupg/[0m

      Public keyring in the image.

    [33m/lib/zfsbootmenu-luks.sh[0m, [33m/lib/zfsbootmenu-luks-keys.sh[0m

      Unlock and teardown functions, and hardware key support.

    [33m/libexec/init.d/45-luks-unlock[0m

      Start-up stage that unlocks volumes.

    [33m/libexec/hooks/teardown.d/80-luks-close.sh[0m

      Teardown hook that exports pools and closes volumes before [1mkexec[0m.

    [33m/zfsbootmenu/luks-mappings[0m, [33m/zfsbootmenu/luks-skipped[0m

      Run-time state: mappings opened by ZFSBootMenu, and volumes skipped by the user.

    [33m/usr/share/examples/zfsbootmenu/crypttab[0m

      Annotated example crypttab, installed with ZFSBootMenu.

  [1mSECURITY[0m

      • Plain key files are never embedded in an image, because images normally live on an
      unencrypted EFI system partition. GnuPG-encrypted key files are embedded. Those
      protected only by a passphrase can be attacked offline by anyone with the image,
      and the build warns about them.
      • A TPM2 token without a PIN unlocks the pools for anyone who can start the computer,
      and ZFSBootMenu offers a recovery shell. Bind such tokens to PCR 7 with Secure
      Boot enabled, or require a PIN.
      • Every vdev that stores pool data must be encrypted: data, special, dedup, log and
      cache vdevs, and spares. An unencrypted log or cache device holds plaintext
      data.
      • Decrypted key material exists only in RAM, below [33m/run[0m, and is removed when unlocking
      ends. GnuPG agents are stopped after each use.

  [1mEXAMPLES[0m

    [1mExample 1: Unlocking only two devices[0m

      The following kernel command line makes ZFSBootMenu unlock only the two partitions of
      the boot pool, ignoring any other LUKS volumes:

      # zbm-kcl -a 'zbm.luks=PARTLABEL=zfs0,PARTLABEL=zfs1' /boot/efi/EFI/zbm/vmlinuz.EFI

    [1mExample 2: Using a key file on a USB stick for every volume[0m

      # zbm-kcl -a 'zbm.luks_key=/keys/pool.key:LABEL=KEYSTICK' /boot/efi/EFI/zbm/vmlinuz.EFI

    [1mExample 3: Disabling LUKS2 tokens for one boot[0m

      At the ZFSBootMenu kernel command-line editor, or in the boot loader, add:

      zbm.luks_tokens=0

    [1mExample 4: Building an image with OpenPGP smart card support[0m

      # cat /etc/zfsbootmenu/dracut.conf.d/luks.conf
      zfsbootmenu_luks_gpg_keys="0x0123456789ABCDEF"
      # generate-zbm

